    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • The Volkswagen Group's Affordable BEV Quartet Has Almost Reached 150,000 Orders\nTwo-Row Mazda CX-90 Purportedly Replaces CX-70 for 2027 in the US\n18 photos\nPhoto: Mazda\nMazda may be replacing the CX-70 in the US with the CX-90 next model year. The catch is that its purported replacement would lose its third row of seats, and the alleged successor would only come in two six-cylinder grades for 2027.\nFrom the standpoint of sales, it sure makes a lot of sense. Through September 2026, year-to-date deliveries totaled 9,686 and 34,999 units, respectively. That's a humongous difference that favors the larger sport utility vehicle in Mazda's lineup for North America, and that's not all. To understand how poorly the CX-70 performed so far in 2026, even the 3 hatchback sold better, moving 11,351 units.\nThe question is, why did the CX-70 fail so hard? There are many different ways to explain this, but most of all, way too similar pricing and weak differentiation over the CX-90 made the larger sibling the superior buy. As of this writing, Mazda's build and price tool for the US market lists 2026 models from $39,300 for the CX-90 and $42,750 for the less practical CX-70, sans destination charge.\nPer the folks at Driving, the company is certain to discontinue the CX-70 stateside even though the US newsroom hasn't been updated with releases for the 2027 models. The cited publication awaits confirmation for the Canadian market regarding a similar change. Looking at the bigger picture, Mazda is most likely to cut its losses across North America rather than the United States market alone.\nFor model year 2027, the purported two-row version of the CX-90 will stop at only two grades: 3.3 Turbo Premium Sport and 3.3 Turbo S Premium Sport. The former trim presently slots above the entry-level 3.3 Turbo Select and the better-equipped 3.3 Turbo Preferred in the US range.\nPhoto: Mazda When equipped with three rows, the 3.3 Turbo Premium Sport retails at $47,480 in the United States of America. The S will set you back a cool $54,140 before taxes and optional extras, but you do get a bit more oomph from the inline-six turbo gasser underhood. The numbers are 280 hp with 332 lb-ft (450 Nm) and 340 hp with 369 lb-ft (500 Nm), respectively.\nMazda recommends 87 regular unleaded for the regular-output motor, whereas the high-output specification takes 91 premium dino juice. Third-row versions of the CX-90 soldier on with seven- or eight-seat layouts, and unfortunately, the plug-in hybrid 2.5 naturally aspirated I4 will only be offered with three rows.\nIn optimal conditions, the plug-in setup develops 323 hp and 369 lb-ft (500 Nm). Electric driving is made possible by a 17.8-kWh battery, which enables a range of 26 miles (42 kilometers). Total range is estimated at 490 miles (789 kilometers), while fuel economy on the four-pot gasoline mill alone is 25 mpg (9.4 l/100 km) in the EPA's combined test cycle.\nBeyond these changes, Driving further understands that Mazda plans a small chassis update for the 2027 model year CX-90. More to the point, a new rear suspension crossmember may be coming for improved passenger comfort. Four new paint colors may be on the menu as well: Zircon Sand Metallic, Aero Grey Metallic, Navy Blue Mica, and Melting Copper Metallic.\nVia // Driving.ca\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2027 Mazda CX-90 2027 Mazda CX-70 Mazda CX-90 mazda cx-70 Mazda\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT An Icon, Reinvented: The Coachman VIP 575 Travel Trailer Is Better Than Ever for 2027",
